{{- /*
io.silverbeer.cycle-runner.plist — launchd agent for cycle-runner (SB-930).

Mac mini only: `.chezmoi.hostname` is the SHORT hostname (no domain — measured
`chezmoi data` on this machine returns "Mac" for a `Mac.fios-router.home` box,
confirming the field, not `.chezmoi.fqdnHostname`, is the right gate). The
whole file, including the XML declaration, sits inside the `if` — chezmoi
treats a template that renders to the empty string as "no target": measured
against a scratch source/dest pair with `chezmoi apply` (both --dry-run and a
real apply), a template that renders empty creates nothing at all, not an
empty file. So on every Mac that is not the mini, this path simply does not
exist after `chezmoi apply` — no `.chezmoiignore` exception needed for that.

`StartCalendarInterval` at :00 and :30 — a 30-minute cadence, matching run.sh's
own "the lock makes a concurrent tick a no-op, never queue two". This was
`StartInterval 1800` until SB-971: an interval timer is a hint launchd may
coalesce, and it deferred this job for 11h and 16h on two consecutive nights
while the machine was awake the whole time. Calendar entries are scheduled
against wall-clock minutes and survive an idle GUI session. `RunAtLoad` false:
the mini's login should not immediately fire a cycle before a human has had a
chance to look at the box.

launchd does not source ~/.zshrc or ~/.zprofile, so PATH here is the explicit
list run.sh's own toolchain needs (op, gh, claude, jq, git, python3): Homebrew
on Apple Silicon, then the user's ~/.local/bin (where `claude` itself lives on
the machine this was built on — ASSUMPTION: the mini's `claude` binary sits in
the same place; doctor.sh's binary check is what catches it if not).
*/ -}}
{{- if eq .chezmoi.hostname "Toms-Mac-mini" -}}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>io.silverbeer.cycle-runner</string>

	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/bash</string>
		<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.claude/skills/cycle-runner/scripts/run.sh</string>
	</array>

	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>HOME</key>
		<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}</string>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
	</dict>

	{{- /* SB-971: StartCalendarInterval, not StartInterval. An interval timer
	   is a hint launchd is free to coalesce, and it defers one aggressively
	   once the GUI session goes idle — measured as 11h and 16h overnight gaps
	   on two consecutive nights, with the machine awake the whole time
	   (`sleep 0` on AC, 50-day uptime, no sleep events in `pmset -g log`).
	   Daytime firing was irregular too: 14:09 to 16:33 in one case.
	   Calendar entries are scheduled against wall-clock minutes instead, so
	   they survive an idle session. Two entries give the same 30-minute
	   cadence run.sh was written for. */}}
	<key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>0</integer>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>Minute</key>
			<integer>30</integer>
		</dict>
	</array>

	{{- /* Background is the honest classification and keeps the job out of the
	   foreground-priority band; it does NOT re-enable the coalescing above,
	   which was a property of StartInterval rather than of process type. */}}
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Background</string>

	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<false/>

	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.local/state/cycle-runner/launchd.out.log</string>

	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>{{ .chezmoi.homeDir }}/.local/state/cycle-runner/launchd.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
{{- end -}}
